<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franciscans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1209">1209</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Order of Friars Minor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Franciscans are the Catholic religious orders that follow the rule of Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, approved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> between 1209 and 1223, chief among them the Order of Friars Minor. Vowed to poverty and preaching, the friars spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and became leading missionaries in the Spanish and Portuguese empires. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> they founded missions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, often building churches and towns among indigenous peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franciscans.jpg" alt="Franciscans" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Piotr Jaworski, PioM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FrancescoCoA_PioM.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a> was the son of a rich cloth merchant who gave up his wealth. In 1209 a sermon on Matthew 10:9 made such an impression on him that he decided to devote himself wholly to apostolic poverty, and he began to preach repentance, barefoot and in a rough garment. Bernard of Quintavalle joined him, and within a year he had 11 companions, who lived in the deserted leper colony of Rivo Torto near Assisi and travelled through the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>. The name Ordo Fratrum Minorum, the Order of Lesser Brothers, expresses his rejection of wealth, and he gave his followers the simple tunic of peasants as their habit.</p><p>The original Rule approved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> did not allow the ownership of property, and the friars begged for food while preaching. Clare of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, under Francis's guidance, founded the Poor Clares, and a Third Order was created for men and women who stayed in secular life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The friars reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> in 1217, and in 1219 Francis went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a> and met the Sultan Malik al-Kamil. The extreme poverty required of members was relaxed in the final revision of the Rule in 1223, and how strictly to observe it became a major source of conflict within the order, with numerous secessions. After 1232 Brother Elias gathered money for a great basilica at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, which alienated the zealots, and in the 14th century the quarrels produced a number of separate congregations. In about 1236 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> appointed the Franciscans, with the Dominicans, as inquisitors, and in 1252 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> approved their use of torture to extract confessions.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> the first three Flemish friars arrived in 1523, and in May 1524 the Twelve Apostles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> came, led by Martín de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>. They built the Convento Grande de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, which was the Franciscan headquarters of New Spain for the next three hundred years. Pedro de Gante began the evangelisation and studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> through the children of the Indian elite of Tetzcoco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The First Order comprises three groups, each with its own minister general: the Friars Minor, called the Observants, the Conventuals, formed in 1209, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capuchins">Capuchins</a>, founded in 1520. In 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a> combined several smaller orders into the present Order of Friars Minor. Franciscans view creation as good, and Francis expressed affection for animals and natural objects in his Canticle of the Creatures. Among the early members were the scholastics Alexander of Hales, Bonaventure and John Duns Scotus, and the scholar Roger Bacon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franciscans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Monasteries became centres of prayer, learning, farming and charity; in the West they preserved classical texts and founded schools, and later religious orders, such as the <b>Franciscans</b> and Dominicans in the 13th century and the Jesuits in the 16th, preached, taught and led missions across the world.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1598 Juan de Oñate led settlers, soldiers and <b>Franciscans</b> north and founded the colony of New Mexico; after Acoma’s warriors killed a party of his soldiers, his forces stormed the mesa in January 1599 and killed several hundred of its people.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Theatines were founded in 1524 by Gaetano and Cardinal Gian Caraffa; the Capuchins, an offshoot of the <b>Franciscans</b> proposed by Matteo de Bascio in 1526, were noted for preaching and care of the poor and sick; the Ursulines were the first order of women devoted to educating…</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote>New orders spread fast: the Cistercians from 1098, and in the 13th century the friars, <b>Franciscans</b> and Dominicans, who preached in the towns and lived by begging.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Franciscans</b> built a great convent on a pyramid at Izamal in the 1550s.</blockquote><a href="/state/ah-kin-chel">Ah Kin Chel</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Franciscans</b> under Junípero Serra founded a mission at San Diego that year, and Monterey, founded in 1770, later became the capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/alta-california">Alta California</a></li></ul></section>
